@* The Castmill mark.

   ONE SOURCE, MANY PIECES — which is the whole product in a glyph: a full-height bar on the
   left is the source master, and three bars fan right at decreasing lengths as the pieces cut
   from it. It replaces a literal millstone (a terracotta disc with eight spokes and a square
   eye) that read as a wagon wheel or a ship's helm at any size, and said nothing about what
   the app does.

   Geometry is on a 456 grid so the same drawing serves the app icon. Content sits inside
   108–348 (~53% of the canvas), comfortably within the ~66% safe area that Android adaptive
   icons and macOS masking crop to — the mark survives a circular mask untouched.

   Colour comes from currentColor and the accent token, so it inherits both theme families
   and both modes rather than carrying its own palette. *@

@* Intrinsic width/height are load-bearing, not decoration: an SVG with only a viewBox
   falls back to filling its container, so if the stylesheet is missing or stale the mark
   blows up and pushes the wordmark onto its own line. CSS overrides these freely. *@
<svg class="cm-mark @Class" viewBox="0 0 456 456" width="24" height="24" role="img"
     aria-label="@Label" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" focusable="false">
    @* The source master: one bar, spanning the full height of the fan beside it. *@
    <rect x="108" y="140" width="40" height="176" rx="20" fill="currentColor" />

    @* The pieces cut from it. The longest carries the accent — the hero output. *@
    <rect x="176" y="140" width="172" height="40" rx="20" fill="var(--cm-accent)" />
    <rect x="176" y="208" width="120" height="40" rx="20" fill="currentColor" opacity="0.85" />
    <rect x="176" y="276" width="76" height="40" rx="20" fill="currentColor" opacity="0.6" />
</svg>

@code {
    /// <summary>Extra class for sizing at the call site; the mark itself is size-agnostic.</summary>
    [Parameter] public string? Class { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// Empty when the mark sits beside the wordmark — the text already names the product, and
    /// a second "Castmill" is noise to a screen reader.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public string Label { get; set; } = "Castmill";
}
